  "content": {
    "id": "p45-q21-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "21",
    "prompt": "Consider the IVP $\\dfrac{dy}{dx}=\\dfrac{2y}{x-1}$ with $y(2)=1$.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p45-q21-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Show that the constant solution of the DE is not a solution of the IVP.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "The constant solution is $y=0$, which gives $y(2)=0\\ne1$.",
          "worked": "For a constant solution, $\\dfrac{dy}{dx}=0$.\n$$\\begin{align*}0&=\\frac{2y}{x-1}\\\\y&=0\\end{align*}$$\nThis gives $y(2)=0\\ne1$, so it is not a solution of the IVP.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 20
      },
      {
        "id": "p45-q21-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Use separation of variables to find the general solution of the DE.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$y=C(x-1)^2$, where $C$ is an arbitrary real constant and $x\\ne1$.",
          "worked": "For $y\\ne0$,\n$$\\begin{align*}\\frac1y\\,dy&=\\frac2{x-1}\\,dx\\\\\\int\\frac1y\\,dy&=\\int\\frac2{x-1}\\,dx\\\\\\ln|y|&=2\\ln|x-1|+C_1\\\\y&=C(x-1)^2\\end{align*}$$\nAllowing $C=0$ includes the constant solution. The DE requires $x\\ne1$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 34
      },
      {
        "id": "p45-q21-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "Hence solve the IVP.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$y=(x-1)^2$, for $x>1$.",
          "worked": "Substitute $x=2$, $y=1$:\n$$\\begin{align*}1&=C(2-1)^2\\\\C&=1\\\\y&=(x-1)^2\\end{align*}$$\nThe interval containing the initial point is $x>1$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 21
      }
    ]
  },
